<p>&#8220;The sight of an old limping horse, tugged forward by one man while another kept beating it with a stick to get it to the knacker&#8217;s yard at Colmar, haunted me for weeks,&#8221; wrote Albert Schweitzer in his 1924 memoir. &#8220;So when my mother had prayed with me and had kissed me good-night, I used to add silently a prayer that I had composed myself for all living creatures. It ran thus: &#8216;O, heavenly Father, protect and bless all things that have breath; guard them from all evil, and let them sleep in peace.'&#8221;</p>
